.\" pr.1 -- maux's pr(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An original
.\" page (this project's own text), so it carries no third-party
.\" notice -- MIT like the code, same posture as env.1/man.1.
.TH PR 1 "October 8, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
pr \- paginate files for printing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pr
[
.BI + page
] [
.BI \- column
] [
.B \-adFmt
] [
.BI \-e [ char ][ gap ]
] [
.BI \-i [ char ][ gap ]
] [
.BI \-h " header"
] [
.BI \-l " lines"
] [
.BI \-n [ char ][ width ]
] [
.BI \-o " offset"
] [
.BI \-s [ char ]
] [
.BI \-w " width"
] [
.I file
.IR .\|.\|. ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pr
writes each
.I file
(standard input if none is given) to standard output, broken
into pages: a 5\-line header
.RI ( date ,
the file's name or
.BR \-h 's
.IR header ,
and a page number), the file's own lines, and enough trailing
blank lines to fill the page \(em even the LAST page of a short
file is padded out to the full page length. Multiple
.I file
operands each start their own page numbering at 1 and name
themselves in their own header; with no operand, the header
names no file at all.
.PP
The header's
.I date
is the file's own last\-modification time (or the current time,
for standard input) \(em never the time
.I pr
itself ran.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-t
Write neither header nor trailer, and never break the output
into pages at all \(em every line is copied straight through.
.TP
.BI \-l " lines"
Set the page length (default 66). At most
.I lines
\-10 lines of file content appear on any one page. A
.I lines
too small to leave room for even one content line under a
header falls back to the
.B \-t
shape (matching this tree's own oracle host, measured live).
.TP
.BI \-h " header"
Replace the file's own name in the header with
.IR header .
.TP
.BI \-n [ char ][ width ]
Number each line of file content, right\-justified in a field
.I width
characters wide (default 5), followed by
.I char
(default a tab). Both are optional and, if given, must be
ATTACHED to the option
.RI ( \-n:3 ,
not
.RI "\-n \ :3)."
.TP
.B \-d
Double\-space the file's content lines.
.TP
.BI \-o " offset"
Indent every output line, header included, by
.I offset
spaces.
.TP
.BI + page
Skip every page before
.IR page ,
without affecting its own page numbering \(em the file is still
read in full.
.TP
.BI \- column
Write
.I column
text columns per page, filled DOWN each column in turn (the
default 1 is the plain layout). A page holds
.I lines
\-10 rows (the page length itself under
.BR \-t );
the last, short page is as short as it can be, left columns
first. Columns are as wide as (width \- (column \- 1)) / column
(default width 72) and a longer line is TRUNCATED to that, the
last column included. Spaces between columns are written as
tabs, as under
.BR \-i .
A page of columns that does not fit in memory (more than 1024
lines, or a text arena of roughly 38 KB) is refused with a
message and exit status 1; nothing is ever written short.
.TP
.B \-a
With
.BI \- column ,
fill each row across before starting the next.
.TP
.B \-m
Merge: one column per
.IR file ,
read in lockstep. At most five real files (a process has eight
file descriptors and three are standard input, output and error);
a sixth is refused before anything is read. A
.B \-
operand is standard input and takes no descriptor. A file that
has run out leaves its cell empty and the others keep their
columns. The header names no file. Not with
.BI \- column .
.TP
.BI \-s [ char ]
Separate columns with
.I char
(default tab) instead of padding. The default width becomes 512.
.TP
.BI \-w " width"
Line width for columns (default 72). Without
.B \-s
at most 254: column positions are kept in 8 bits.
.TP
.BI \-e [ char ][ gap ]
Expand input tabs (or
.IR char )
to every
.I gap
th column (default 8) before the line is cut into columns.
.TP
.BI \-i [ char ][ gap ]
Write runs of two or more spaces as tabs (or
.IR char )
at every
.I gap
th column (default 8); a lone space stays a space and spaces
at the end of a line are dropped. Assumed for more than one
column.
.TP
.B \-F
Page boundaries are a form feed in place of the blank padding
lines. No effect under
.BR \-t .
.SH FILES
.br
/usr/man/PR  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cat(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success, 1 if a file could not be opened or
a limit above was exceeded, 2 for a usage error.
.SH BUGS
A short last page of columns is filled left first (4, 4, 2 for
ten lines in three), as BSD does; GNU balances (4, 3, 3). Under
.B \-n
every column cell is numbered; BSD and GNU lay the number out
differently from each other and from this. A file's modification
date is real wall\-clock time, so two runs of the same command
print different headers by design.
